# Trivy CVE allowlist for HaraLedger service images.
#
# Each entry must justify WHY the CVE is not exploitable AND name the
# unblocking action. This file is scanned at every push
# (.github/workflows/services.yml Trivy step) so anything ignored here
# is being deliberately deferred.
#
# Net allowlist as of 2026-05-22 (post distroless multi-stage migration):
#   6 entries total. Down from 19 pre-distroless.
#
# What changed: the multi-stage build now produces a
# gcr.io/distroless/nodejs22-debian12 runtime image with no pnpm, no
# npm, no corepack, no shell. The previous ~13 allowlisted CVEs in
# build-time tooling (cross-spawn / glob / picomatch / minimatch /
# node-tar / pnpm internals / npm-bundled deps) are GONE from the
# runtime image. Only CVEs in actual app dependencies + the distroless
# base remain.
#
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# libssl3 in the distroless base image (debian 12.13)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 6 CVEs (1 CRITICAL + 5 HIGH) in libssl3@3.0.18-1~deb12u2 — Google's
# distroless/nodejs22-debian12 base image. Five relate to OpenSSL's
# DTLS handling or TLS 1.3 corner cases; CVE-2026-45447 (added 2026-07-29)
# is a heap use-after-free in PKCS7_verify() (fixed in 3.0.20-1~deb12u2).
#
# Why we're not exposed:
#   1. Internal: services talk to each other over the WireGuard mesh
#      (ChaCha20-Poly1305), not TLS. libssl3 is loaded by Node but
#      our code never opens a TLS server or makes outbound TLS calls
#      to anything that uses DTLS.
#   2. External: Caddy on hara-stateless terminates all public TLS
#      using its own openssl stack, not the service image's libssl.
#      Internal services bind on 127.0.0.1 / mesh IPs only.
#
# Unblock: Google updates distroless base images monthly; rebuild
# unconditionally clears these when a new deb12u3+ tag is published.
# Track: https://github.com/GoogleContainerTools/distroless/releases
CVE-2026-31789
CVE-2026-28387
CVE-2026-28388
CVE-2026-28389
CVE-2026-28390
CVE-2026-45447

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# @opentelemetry/exporter-prometheus (transitively pulled by sdk-node)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CVE-2026-44902 — Prometheus exporter process crash via malformed
# HTTP request.
#
# Why we're not exploitable:
#   We use OTLP/HTTP exporter exclusively (configured in
#   services/shared/src/otel.ts pointing at Tempo at $OTEL_EXPORTER_
#   OTLP_ENDPOINT). The Prometheus exporter ships with
#   @opentelemetry/sdk-node as a peer/optional dep but is never
#   instantiated — there's no `new PrometheusExporter()` anywhere in
#   the codebase. The vulnerable HTTP handler can only crash if a
#   PrometheusExporter instance is created AND bound to a port AND
#   reached by a malformed request. None of those conditions hold.
#
# Unblock: bump @opentelemetry/sdk-node when the fixed version
# (>= 0.206.0 likely) lands.
CVE-2026-44902
